course: dictionary
lesson: 1508
title: Celery: a vegetable, a stem, and a sound
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Celery is a noun, a word for a thing. It is a vegetable with long, pale-green, crunchy stalks and leafy tops, and you can eat it raw or cooked. We say some celery, or a stick of celery, because celery is uncountable.
explain: The sentences give you clues. «Carrots and beetroot are roots, celery and asparagus are stems, lettuce and spinach are leaves...» Celery sits in a list of plant parts, next to stems. Then «stand a celery stick in blue food colouring overnight» tells you that a stick is a piece of the plant you can stand in water.
explain: Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence. In «Foley artists... snap celery in a studio», the celery is not for eating. It makes a crunch that sounds like a bone or a footstep. Ask: does my meaning still make sense here? A snapped vegetable makes a crunch, so it fits.
fact: Celery is a vegetable with long pale-green crunchy stalks and leafy tops.
fact: Celery is uncountable, so we say some celery or a stick of celery.
fact: In the science trick, celery in blue colouring shows how water travels up tubes in the stem, called xylem.
fact: In the sentence «celery and asparagus are stems», celery is an example of a plant stem.
fact: In the UK and Europe, celery must be listed on food labels because some people are allergic to it.
exercise: What is celery? | A kind of bird that lives in a nest | A fruit that grows on trees | *A vegetable with long, pale-green, crunchy stalks | A tool for cutting paper
exercise: «Carrots and beetroot are roots, celery and asparagus are stems...» Which group is celery in? | Roots | *Stems | Leaves | Seeds
exercise: «...snap celery in a studio» Why do Foley artists snap celery? | Celery is a plant, so it helps grow the film | To paint the studio walls | To make a salad for the crew's lunch | *To make a crunchy sound for a film
test: Is celery countable or uncountable? | *Uncountable, so we say some celery | Neither, because it is not a noun | Countable, so we say three celerys | Countable, so we say two celeries
test: «Try the celery trick» with blue food colouring. Why do the leaves turn blue? | *Water is pulled up thin tubes in the stem, like a drink up a straw | Celery is naturally blue | The colour is painted on afterwards | The stick soaks up ink from the sun
exam: Which sentence about celery is correct? | *Celery is a vegetable with long pale-green crunchy stalks and leafy tops that you can eat raw or cooked | Celery is a bird that lives in trees | Celery is a vegetable with long pale-green crunchy stalks | Celery is a fruit with a hard red skin
